    "f_appearance": "",
    "f_personality": "",
    "f_history": "Kakita Sairyū is a 20 year old prodigy of the blade.  He is currently a new teacher of the Kakita Dueling Academy with great potential.  With several duels under his belt and a few successful missions as a samurai he has built quite the reputation for himself as a young up and coming warrior and duelist.\n\nBy age 20 he was appointed as an instructor at the Dueling Academy, shortly after his Genpaku, making him one of the youngest teachers at the academy.\n\nIn his youth he fell in love with his childhood friend Kakita Kaguhana,  Kaguhana was a beautiful young woman that was a fellow duelist.  She could keep step with the best of them and even won a few sparing matches against Sairyū.\n\nIn their teenage years they started a romantic relationship but kept it under wraps, not wanting to spread gossip and let it get in the way of their ambitions for becoming top duelists.\n\nUnfortunately while out on a mission in Lion Territory together, Kaguhana was challenged to a duel by a Lion Samurai (insert Name).  Sairyū could tell that Kaguhana was out matched and out skilled, so he tried to persuade Kaguhana to decline the duel.  However, Kaguhana did not head his warning and proceeded to Duel (Lion Samurai) and alas Kaguhana fell, her life tragically cut short. \n\nFrom that point on Sairyū has vowed to be better and do better.  He strives to not fall victim to the same pride and overconfidence that overtook and consumed his beloved.\n\nSairyū would have loved nothing more in that moment to avenge his love and challenge the Lion Samurai to a duel.  But he abstained, he recognised that he was in no frame of mind to fight, and that if he did he would lose.  He would lose so spectacularly that it wouldn't be worth calling a duel as it would be such a pitiful display of his skill with the blade.\n\nSairyū instead took this opportunity to return home, lay his beloved to rest and continue with his mission, but he never forgot the man who slew Kaguhana.\n\nSairyū has also endeavored to instill this new philosophy into his students.  He is tired of seeing young and promising samurai throw their lives away in pointless duels fueled only by ego and pride.  A duel is a sacred thing and a samurai’s life is a valuable thing.  A samurai is a weapon of the emperor, a tool to be used.  A samurai should not throw their life away so easily.\n\n",
    "f_notes": ""
